First, we show that $k \ge 2n$. Consider the $2n \times 2n$ board and divide it into four $n \times n$ quadrants. Let $k$ be the minimum number of cells that can be marked so that there exists a unique partition of the board into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells. Then we may assume that the marked cells lie in the top-left quadrant, as otherwise we can rotate the board by $180^\circ$ to obtain a configuration with the same number of marked cells where the marked cells lie in the top-left quadrant.

Consider the $n \times n$ grid obtained by removing the top-left quadrant from the board. If we partition the $2n \times 2n$ board into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells, then there exists a partition of the $n \times n$ grid into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells. Indeed, each domino in the partition of the $2n \times 2n$ board either lies entirely in the $n \times n$ grid or crosses the boundary between the top-left quadrant and the $n \times n$ grid. If a domino crosses the boundary, then it contains exactly one marked cell, so we can remove it from the partition and obtain a partition of the $n \times n$ grid into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells.

Let $k'$ be the minimum number of cells that can be marked in an $n \times n$ grid so that there exists a unique partition of the grid into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells. Then $k \ge k'$. We claim that $k' \ge n$. Indeed, let the marked cells be $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_{k'}$ and let the unique partition of the $n \times n$ grid into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells, be $D_1, D_2, \dots, D_m$. Since each domino contains exactly one marked cell, we have $m = k'$. Since the $n \times n$ grid contains $n^2$ cells, we have $2m = 2k' = 2n^2$. Thus $k' = n^2$. However, since each marked cell lies in a unique domino, we have $k' \le n$. Therefore $k' = n$ and $k \ge n$.

Now we show that $k \le 2n$. Consider the $2n \times 2n$ board and mark the cells $(i, 2n - i + 1)$ for $1 \le i \le 2n$. Then we can partition the board into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells, by pairing the cells $(i, j)$ and $(2n - i + 1, 2n - j + 1)$ for $1 \le i < j \le 2n$ and pairing the cells $(i, 2n - i + 1)$ and $(2n - i + 1, i)$ for $1 \le i \le 2n$. It is easy to check that this is the unique partition of the board into dominoes, none of which contains two marked cells. Therefore $k \le 2n$.

Combining the inequalities $k \ge 2n$ and $k \le 2n$, we conclude that $k = 2n$.

