question:
Determine all pairs of angles $(x, y)$ with $0^{\circ} \leq x<180^{\circ}$ and $0^{\circ} \leq y<180^{\circ}$ that satisfy the following system of equations:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\log _{2}(\sin x \cos y) & =-\frac{3}{2} \\
\log _{2}\left(\frac{\sin x}{\cos y}\right) & =\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

response:
We know that the right hand side of the given equation is equal to $0+0+1+1+2+2+\ldots+\left\lfloor\frac{m-2}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{m-1}{3}\right\rfloor$.  The sequence $0,0,1,1,2,2,\ldots$ repeats with period 3, so the sum of any three consecutive terms is equal to 4.  Thus, the sum of the first $3k$ terms is equal to $4k$ for every positive integer $k$.  Since $m-1$ is a positive integer, we can write $m-1=3k+r$ for some nonnegative integer $r<3$.  Then

\begin{align*}
\left\lfloor\frac{1}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{2}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{3}{3}\right\rfloor+\ldots+\left\lfloor\frac{m-2}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{m-1}{3}\right\rfloor&=4k+\left\lfloor\frac{3k+1}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{3k+2}{3}\right\rfloor\\
&=4k+k+k\\
&=5k.
\end{align*}

Since $m=3k+r+1$, we have $k=\frac{m-r-1}{3}$.  Thus,

\begin{align*}
\left\lfloor\frac{1}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{2}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{3}{3}\right\rfloor+\ldots+\left\lfloor\frac{m-2}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{m-1}{3}\right\rfloor&=5\cdot\frac{m-r-1}{3}\\
&=\frac{5m-5r-5}{3}.
\end{align*}

If $r=0$, then $m=3k+1$ for some positive integer $k$, so $m\equiv1\pmod 3$.  In this case,

\begin{align*}
\lfloor p(m)\rfloor&=\frac{5m-5r-5}{3}\\
&=\frac{5m-5}{3}.
\end{align*}

If $r=1$, then $m=3k+2$ for some nonnegative integer $k$, so $m\equiv2\pmod 3$.  In this case,

\begin{align*}
\lfloor p(m)\rfloor&=\frac{5m-5r-5}{3}\\
&=\frac{5m-10}{3}.
\end{align*}

If $r=2$, then $m=3k+3$ for some positive integer $k$, so $m\equiv0\pmod 3$.  In this case,

\begin{align*}
\lfloor p(m)\rfloor&=\frac{5m-5r-5}{3}\\
&=\frac{5m-15}{3}.
\end{align*}

Thus, $p(m)$ must be equal to $\frac{5m-5}{3}$ when $m\equiv1\pmod 3$, equal to $\frac{5m-10}{3}$ when $m\equiv2\pmod 3$, and equal to $\frac{5m-15}{3}$ when $m\equiv0\pmod 3$.  If we let $p(x)=\frac{5x-10}{3}$, then $p(m)=\frac{5m-10}{3}$ when $m\equiv2\pmod 3$, so $\lfloor p(m)\rfloor=\lfloor\frac{5m-10}{3}\rfloor=\frac{5m-10}{3}$.  When $m\equiv1\pmod 3$, we have

\begin{align*}
\lfloor p(m)\rfloor&=\left\lfloor\frac{5m-10}{3}\right\rfloor\\
&=\left\lfloor\frac{5m-5}{3}-\frac{5}{3}\right\rfloor\\
&=\frac{5m-5}{3}+\left\lfloor-\frac{5}{3}\right\rfloor\\
&=\frac{5m-5}{3}-2\\
&=\frac{5m-11}{3}.
\end{align*}

When $m\equiv0\pmod 3$, we have

\begin{align*}
\lfloor p(m)\rfloor&=\left\lfloor\frac{5m-10}{3}\right\rfloor\\
&=\left\lfloor\frac{5m-15}{3}+\frac{5}{3}\right\rfloor\\
&=\frac{5m-15}{3}+\left\lfloor\frac{5}{3}\right\rfloor\\
&=\frac{5m-15}{3}+1\\
&=\frac{5m-12}{3}.
\end{align*}

Since $p(x)=\frac{5x-10}{3}$, we have $p(m)=\frac{5m-10}{3}$ for every positive integer $m>4$.  Therefore, $p(x)=\boxed{\frac{5x-10}{3}}$ is the polynomial we are looking for.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{\frac{5x-10}{3}}</answer>

Taking the logarithm of both sides of the equation $t=a m^{b}$ gives

\begin{align*}
\log t&=\log a + b \log m.
\end{align*}

Thus, $\log t$ is a linear function of $\log m$ with slope $b$ and $y$-intercept $\log a$.  We can determine $b$ and $\log a$ by taking the points $(\log 3.00, \log 2.75)$ and $(\log 6.00, \log 3.75)$ and finding the slope of the line through these points:

\begin{align*}
b&=\frac{\log 3.75-\log 2.75}{\log 6.00-\log 3.00} \\
&=\log\left(\frac{3.75}{2.75}\right) \div \log 2 \\
&\approx 0.30.
\end{align*}

The $y$-intercept is

\begin{align*}
\log a&=\log 2.75-0.30 \cdot \log 3.00 \\
&\approx -0.11,
\end{align*}

so $a=10^{-0.11} \approx 0.78$.  Therefore, $a \approx 0.78$ and $b \approx 0.30$.
